================================================================================
PLAIN-ENGLISH USAGE GUIDE & TERMS OF USE
================================================================================

This section provides a clear, human-readable summary of your rights, guidelines,
and best practices when using, sharing, or developing UnCapped. This guide is
intended to help you understand how you can use this application. The legal terms
above remain the governing license.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. WHAT YOU ARE FREE TO DO
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

✔ Free for Everyone:
  You can download, install, and run UnCapped completely free of charge for personal,
  educational, research, or commercial purposes.

✔ Modify and Customize:
  You can modify the source code, tweak themes and UI styling, change connection
  parameters, add new features, or adapt it to your specific workflows.

✔ Share and Distribute:
  You can freely share installers (Windows .exe, macOS .app, Linux bundles) or the
  source code with colleagues, friends, family, or your organization.

✔ Integrate and Bundle:
  You can include UnCapped or its components in other projects, internal tools,
  or commercial distributions, provided you include the copyright notice and license.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. YOUR RESPONSIBILITIES & CONDITIONS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

• Keep the Copyright Notice:
  If you distribute copies of the software or forks of the code, you must include the
  original copyright notice ("Copyright (c) 2026 Anmol Kapil") and the MIT License text.

• No Warranty ("As Is"):
  The software is provided without any warranty. The creators do not guarantee that the
  software will always be uninterrupted, bug-free, or compatible with every network adapter.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. RESPONSIBLE & ACCEPTABLE USE GUIDELINES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

When running UnCapped, please follow these guidelines:

1. Download Authorization & Copyright Respect:
   Only download files, media, software, and content that you are legally authorized
   to access and download. UnCapped is a neutral, high-performance transfer accelerator
   tool; users are solely responsible for ensuring their downloads comply with all
   applicable local, national, and international laws.

2. Mobile Data & ISP Plan Awareness:
   UnCapped bonds multiple network connections simultaneously (such as Wi-Fi + 5G/4G
   USB tethering + Ethernet). Because work is distributed across all active adapters:
   - Data consumed on tethered devices counts toward your carrier data plans and hot-spot limits.
   - You are responsible for monitoring your data allowances and any potential carrier fees
     or overage charges.
   - Use the built-in interface toggle switches in UnCapped to disable any metered connections
     you do not wish to use for a particular transfer.

3. Server Etiquette & Rate Limits:
   UnCapped utilizes multi-stream concurrent HTTP Range requests (up to 8 streams per
   interface). Ensure destination servers permit multi-connection downloading and respect
   any explicit rate limits or Terms of Service imposed by remote hosts.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. FREQUENTLY ASKED QUESTIONS (FAQ)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Q: Do I need to pay any license fees or royalties to use UnCapped?
A: No. UnCapped is 100% free and open-source under the MIT License.

Q: Can I use UnCapped in a commercial or corporate office setting?
A: Yes. Commercial use is explicitly permitted without fees.

Q: Can I fork this repository and release my own modified version?
A: Yes. You can fork, rename, add new features, and distribute your modified version,
   as long as you include the original MIT license notice.

Q: Who is responsible if I run into carrier data overage charges?
A: The user is solely responsible for network usage and carrier costs. As stated in the
   warranty disclaimer, authors and contributors accept no liability for data costs,
   hardware issues, or third-party server actions.
